 my games goddammit. <i>Ugh.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-10T15:44:29-07:00","id":1507969,"title":"","content":"While I hate EA's practices in general, I have to say their new client has been less buggy for me than Origin was... but I haven't tried running older games on the new client yet so I might just be lucking out there. Of course, you have to wonder how many backroom deals are getting made between hardware companies and software companies, because it feels like part of the push to make sure you have half a dozen clients running in the background, is so even what seemed like a decent high end gaming PC will slow to a crawl from all the extra shit gaming companies are loading on to your system (also Discord, Steam/GoG/etc, Microsoft, Twitch, and your graphics card all want to run overlays on your games too!). It feels like they just want to force us to keep buying new computers or new RAM chips and new GPUs constantly... even for games that have been out for years.<br /> <br /> <br /> The whole thing is crap. I buy a lot of physical copies of games precisely because I want to be be able to able to access the games as I bought them, so anything that tries to punish us for that is annoying, if not extremely shady.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theon