{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-14T12:52:11-07:00","id":1508378,"title":"","content":"Once upon a time, I ran a sandbox-style tabletop D&amp;D game in a homebrew world that would be called an isekai-avatar (or self-insert) game in modern terms. I, like many older players, remember the 80s D&amp;D cartoon and recognize the premise as being the same. From the responses of my players, the fact that my playerbase rose to around 13 (this was during college), and the fact that the game\u2019s ultimate duration was measured in years, I gather that the game itself was enjoyable. But I find that while I could run such a game for others, none of them were able to run such a game I could play in. They just couldn\u2019t keep up with the details even with assistance, or harbored psychological or ideological issues with the genre that poisoned the game, or misfortune befell them whenever they might try running one of their own. There is no conceivable way I could be doing anything unique just running things straight out of the books with a base concept introduced in 1983, so it became frustrating that I could never experience what my players did.<br /> <br /> Now I find that the ubiquitous connectivity of the current era, and the apparent wealth of isekai-genre anime, has people thinking about the type of game I used to run some 15-20 years later. Reading through their comments and brainstorming; I see the same logistic issues I\u2019ve already dealt with decades ago, the same trollish skepticism that I have already proven unfounded, with some of the same ideas arising and aborting with the blinding speed of the internet, and my frustration rises again. I wonder if the universe is deliberately mocking me.<br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/765-darksol-aeternium","name":"Darksol-aeternium","avatar":"https://foru